<h2>What Are Agents?</h2>

<p>
    Think of agents as your personal assistants for repetitive tasks. Instead of manually:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Opening multiple pages</li>
    <li>Copying and pasting information</li>
    <li>Filling out forms</li>
    <li>Sending the same emails over and over</li>
</ul>

<p>
    You create an agent once that does all of this automatically. When you need it, you just click a button or ask in chat, and the agent handles the rest.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Visual comparison: Left side shows manual steps (tedious, time-consuming), right side shows agent automation (one click, instant). Use icons and arrows to show the difference. -->

<h2>Where Agents Show Up</h2>

<p>
    Agents appear in two main places so you can run them right where you work:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Action buttons on key pages</strong> – Agents appear as buttons on relevant pages:
        <ul>
            <li>Client policy lists</li>
            <li>Renewal submissions</li>
            <li>Renewal task lists</li>
            <li>Client quick tools</li>
            <li>Other pages where the agent makes sense</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Enhanced AI Chat</strong> - Type what you want (for example, "Send a renewal reminder") and chat can route your request to the best agent automatically</li>
</ul>

<p>
    This means you don't have to remember where agents are or how to run them—they're available exactly when and where you need them.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of a client page showing an "Agent Actions" panel with a few agent buttons placed near the policy list. Also show a chat interface with a user typing "Send payment link" and the chat suggesting an agent. -->

<h2>Creating and Editing Agents</h2>

<p>
    You manage custom agents from the menu:
</p>

<ol>
    <li>Open <strong>Profile → Custom Agents</strong></li>
    <li>Choose an existing agent to edit, or click <strong>Create New</strong> to start fresh</li>
    <li>Configure the agent:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Name</strong> – Give it a clear, descriptive name (e.g., "Send Renewal Reminder" not "Agent1")</li>
            <li><strong>Icon</strong> - Choose an icon that visually represents what the agent does</li>
            <li><strong>Category</strong> - Organize agents by category (Email, Renewals, etc.)</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong> – Write a short explanation of when and why to use this agent</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Define <strong>parameters</strong> – Specify what information the agent needs (client, policy, contact, etc.)</li>
    <li>Configure <strong>actions</strong> – Set up what the agent will do (send email, open page, etc.)</li>
    <li>Assign <strong>zones</strong> – Choose where the agent should appear (which pages)</li>
    <li>Save and publish the agent</li>
</ol>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the agent designer "Basic Information" section showing fields for Name, Icon selector, Category dropdown, Outcome type, and Description. Show the form with example values filled in. -->

<h2>Publishing and Using Agents</h2>

<p>
    Once your agent is set up:
</p>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Save and publish</strong> it from the Custom Agents screen</li>
    <li>The agent will <strong>immediately appear</strong> in its assigned action zones</li>
    <li>It becomes <strong>available to Enhanced AI Chat</strong> for automatic routing</li>
    <li>Run it from:
        <ul>
            <li>Action buttons on pages</li>
            <li>By asking in chat (for example, "Run the Welcome Email agent for Acme Manufacturing")</li>
            <li>From the agent list in Profile → Custom Agents</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ol>

<p>
    Changes to agents take effect immediately—no need to restart or redeploy anything.
</p>

